{0 Rocq/OCaml Adequacy}

This audit compares the active Kairos implementation with the frozen POPL
PaperCore proof principle.  It does not prescribe the OCaml architecture from
the organization of the Rocq files.

{1 Current Verdict}

The current claim is deliberately limited:

{[
POPL PaperCore formalizes the intended assume-guarantee proof principle.
Kairos constructs related product summaries and proof contracts.
Full adequacy of the current OCaml implementation is not yet proved.
]}

The active OCaml pipeline is the implementation reference.  POPL is the Rocq
reference used to state and assess the mathematical correspondence.

{1 Direct Correspondence}

{2 Program and Execution}

- Rocq transition systems correspond to normalized
  [Verification_model.node_model] values.
- Program states, transitions, guards and bodies are represented directly in
  [Verification_model] and [Ir].
- Source syntax elaboration is outside the frozen proof.

Status: partial.  The core shapes correspond, but there is no mechanized
correctness theorem for the frontend elaboration.

{2 Supplied Automata}

- Rocq assumes contract automata as parameters.
- Kairos consumes [Automaton_types.automata_spec] and validates the normal form
  required by product construction.
- Spot generates those automata outside the reference kernel.

Status: adequate relative to supplied automata; Spot correctness is outside
the theorem.

{2 Product States and Steps}

- [Product_types.product_state] and [Product_types.product_step] carry the
  program, assumption and guarantee components used by the implementation.
- [Product_build] and [From_model] construct the explored product.

Status: partial.  POPL reasons over its mathematical product, whereas OCaml
uses structural reachable exploration.  Their extensional correspondence is
not yet a theorem.

{2 Product Summaries}

[Ir.product_step_summary] is the single active implementation carrier.  It
contains:

- trace and program-transition identity;
- product source and assumption guard;
- propagation requirements and ordinary requirements;
- elaboration checks and ensures;
- safe and unsafe product cases.

[Pre], [Product_reachability] and [Post] enrich these summaries before
[Temporal_lower] materializes historical slots.

Status: partial.  These fields expose the information needed by the active
implementation, but no separate OCaml copy of Rocq [KernelClause],
[TimedFact] or [SummaryClauseFamilies] is maintained.  Such a copy would not
prove correspondence.

{2 Proof Contracts}

[Step_contract_projection] constructs the contracts actually used for proof:

- safe contracts from admissible successors;
- bad-guarantee contracts from excluded unsafe cases;
- preconditions, postconditions and exclusions;
- stable transition and product-source identity.

[Proof_plan] attaches partition provenance, then performs prover-independent
grouping, factorization and sharing. Why3 translates the resulting plan
without redefining the obligations.

Status: partial.  The active implementation contract set is explicit, but its
equivalence with the generated-obligation predicate of POPL has not been
mechanized.

{2 Proof Discharge}

Why3 and external provers discharge the planned contracts.  Their encodings,
helper structure, scheduling, diagnostics and solver results are outside the
POPL theorem.

Status: outside Rocq, with a required preservation argument from
[Step_contract_projection] through [Proof_plan] to generated verification
conditions.

{1 Known Gaps}

The main remaining proof obligations are:

1. relate OCaml reachable product exploration to the product quantified by
   POPL;
2. relate the formulas constructed by [Pre], [Product_reachability] and [Post]
   to the local invariant hypotheses used by POPL;
3. prove that [Step_contract_projection] represents exactly the implementation
   obligations required by the intended proof principle;
4. prove that [Proof_plan] grouping, factorization and sharing preserve those
   obligations;
5. characterize initialization obligations: [Ir.init_invariant_goals] is not
   currently active proof content and must not be presented as already
   discharged;
6. keep frontend elaboration, Spot construction and backend encoding outside
   the theorem unless separate adequacy results are added.

{1 Consequence for Architecture}

No missing theorem is repaired by adding an unused OCaml mirror of a Rocq
record.  Adequacy must be established over the objects the implementation
actually constructs:

{[
Ir.product_step_summary
  -> Step_contract_projection.step_contract
  -> Proof_plan.t
]}

This keeps the scientific gap explicit without letting the artificial
decomposition of the proof dictate the implementation.
